---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const slug = 'openai-navier-stokes-agent-cost-2026';
const date = '2026-09-09';
const locale = 'en';

const enTitle = 'OpenAI Navier-Stokes: 4.9M Agents, $15M Tokens';
const enDescription = 'OpenAI Navier-Stokes proof: 4.9M messages, 300B output tokens, ~10K agents, $15M+ at GPT-6 Astra API rates. Verified breakdown.';
const canonicalUrl = `https://apirank.vip/tutorials/${slug}`;
const zhCanonicalUrl = `https://apirank.vip/zh/tutorials/${slug}`;

const articleJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": enTitle,
  "description": enDescription,
  "datePublished": date,
  "dateModified": date,
  "inLanguage": "en-US",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "APIRank", "url": "https://apirank.vip" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "APIRank", "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://apirank.vip/favicon.svg" } },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonicalUrl }
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://apirank.vip" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Tutorials", "item": "https://apirank.vip/tutorials" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": enTitle, "item": canonicalUrl }
  ]
};
const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How much did OpenAI's Navier-Stokes proof cost in API tokens?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Per OpenAI's own announcement (Sept 8, 2026), the agent group attempting all problems sent 4.9 million messages and used about 300 billion output tokens; the Navier-Stokes resolution alone involved on the order of 10,000 concurrent agents. If you priced the output tokens at GPT-6 Astra's published rate ($50 per 1M tokens on OpenRouter as of 2026-09-09), 300B output tokens alone would be roughly $15M. Noam Brown confirmed the proof cost millions of dollars; the input token bill and the cost of the unreleased internal model are not disclosed." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What model did OpenAI use for the Navier-Stokes proof?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Two distinct models, per OpenAI's announcement. The proof itself was generated by a next-generation OpenAI model described as significantly more capable than GPT-6 Astra, not yet released to the API. GPT-6 Astra (the $10/$50 model launched Sept 5, 2026) was used to perform the Lean 4 formal verification, a step that took about 17 hours of the ~88-hour total workflow. The same agent group had attempted multiple problems before the Navier-Stokes resolution; 4.9M messages and 300B output tokens cover all of those attempts, not just the winning one." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Who else was working on the same Navier-Stokes blow-up problem?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "NYU mathematician Tristan Buckmaster and Anthropic researcher Alpöge had been using AI to work on fluid dynamics problems for over a year. In mid-August 2026 they found a counterexample for a simpler version of the Navier-Stokes problem; in subsequent weeks both groups independently worked on the full blow-up problem. Buckmaster posted a public statement (cims.nyu.edu/~tristanb/statement.pdf, Sept 7) describing the timing and coordination attempt. Terrence Tao commented on the math via Mastodon (mathstodon.xyz/@tao/117233527638291447). Sam Altman publicly responded (Sept 8) claiming the Anthropic side only had the Euler result and that coordination had failed, and accused them of a plagiarism threat." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How does this affect the price of running agent workloads on OpenAI's API?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "It does not change the published price sheet. GPT-6 Astra still costs $10/$50 per 1M tokens on OpenRouter as of 2026-09-09; the batch tier halves both to $5/$25. The takeaway is operational, not pricing: large multi-agent research workloads cost seven figures even at the published rates, and input tokens (charged at the prompt rate) can be a similar order of magnitude when agents read large proof contexts. Noam Brown's prediction that cost will fall fast is the relevant trend signal — agent cost economics are on a similar trajectory to inference cost economics in 2024-25." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What does the Navier-Stokes solution actually prove?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The Clay Millennium Prize problem on Navier-Stokes asks whether smooth initial conditions for the 3D incompressible Navier-Stokes equations can develop a singularity (blow-up) in finite time. OpenAI's announcement says their agent group produced a blow-up solution, i.e. an example showing smooth flow can become singular. The proof is presented with a Lean 4 formal verification completed by GPT-6 Astra, which is a stronger guarantee than a human-written proof because every step is machine-checked. Independent mathematical assessment from Terrence Tao and others is still pending; the proof text is hosted on OpenAI's site alongside the announcement." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is the OpenAI Navier-Stokes proof a real scientific breakthrough or a marketing story?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Both. On the math side, a verified Lean 4 blow-up solution would be a substantive result — it answers the Millennium Prize question in one direction — but the community has not yet finished independent review, and Buckmaster's statement raises procedural concerns about credit and coordination. On the engineering side, the announcement is a public benchmark for agent capability and cost at frontier scale: 4.9M messages, 300B output tokens, ~10K concurrent agents, ~88 hours end-to-end. For API consumers, the practical signal is that 'frontier research with agents' is now a real product tier — and it costs seven figures per problem at current rates." } }
  ]
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title={enTitle + ' | APIRank'}
  description={enDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  zhCanonicalUrl={zhCanonicalUrl}
  type="article"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleJsonLd)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />

  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en-US" href={canonicalUrl} />
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="zh-CN" href={zhCanonicalUrl} />
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href={canonicalUrl} />

  <div class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-3">
      <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
        <span>›</span>
        <a href="/tutorials" class="hover:text-blue-600">Tutorials</a>
        <span>›</span>
        <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">OpenAI Navier-Stokes Agent Cost</span>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="py-8">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
        <div class="lg:col-span-2 space-y-8">

          <header>
            <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">OpenAI's Navier-Stokes Proof: 4.9M Messages, ~10K Agents, and a $15M Token Bill</h1>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
              <time datetime="2026-09-09">September 9, 2026</time>
              <span>•</span>
              <span>News Analysis</span>
              <span>•</span>
              <span>About 11 min read</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              On September 8, 2026, OpenAI published a paper, a Lean 4 formal verification, and a 1,103-point Hacker News thread claiming an AI agent group had solved the Navier-Stokes blow-up Millennium Prize problem. The math story is the headline, but the more durable signal for APIRank readers is the one in the announcement's small print: <strong>4.9 million messages, about 300 billion output tokens, ~10,000 concurrent agents, ~88 hours end-to-end</strong>. Priced at GPT-6 Astra's published API rates, that workload already clears <strong>$15M in output tokens alone</strong> — before counting input tokens, the unreleased internal model's cost, or the human team that organized the run. This piece is the verified breakdown of the cost economics, the two-model architecture (unreleased frontier model for proof + GPT-6 Astra for verification), and the concurrent-work dispute with NYU's Tristan Buckmaster and Anthropic's Alpöge.
            </p>
          </header>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-400 p-4 rounded-r">
            <p class="text-blue-800 text-sm">
              <strong>TL;DR:</strong> OpenAI's agent group sent 4.9M messages and used ~300B output tokens across all attempted problems (not just the winning one); the Navier-Stokes resolution alone ran ~10K concurrent agents for ~88 hours; GPT-6 Astra performed the 17-hour Lean 4 verification. At GPT-6 Astra's $50/M output rate on OpenRouter, the output bill alone is ~$15M — Noam Brown confirms the run cost "millions of dollars." The proof used an <strong>unreleased next-gen OpenAI model</strong> (significantly stronger than GPT-6 Astra); GPT-6 Astra is just the verification step. NYU's Tristan Buckmaster and Anthropic's Alpöge were working on the same problem independently; Sam Altman's Sept 8 response accused the Anthropic side of threatening a plagiarism complaint. For API consumers, the practical signal is that frontier research-grade agent workloads now cost seven figures per problem at current rates, and the input-token half of the bill is not disclosed.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What OpenAI actually announced</h2>

          <p>OpenAI's announcement (Sept 8, 2026) — a paper hosted at <code>openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/</code>, cross-posted to HN where it drew 1,103 points and 955 comments in 24 hours — describes a single agent group that worked on multiple mathematical problems before producing a verified Navier-Stokes blow-up solution. The relevant quoted numbers, in OpenAI's own words:</p>

          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
            <li><strong>"Across all attempted problems, the agents sent 4.9 million messages and used about 300 billion output tokens."</strong> — this is the cumulative bill for the multi-problem campaign, not just the winning proof.</li>
            <li><strong>"The group that produced the Navier-Stokes resolution involved on the order of 10,000 concurrent agents."</strong> — top HN commenter <em>seizethecheese</em> pulled this line directly from the announcement.</li>
            <li><strong>The full workflow ran ~88 hours end-to-end</strong>; the Lean 4 formal verification took ~17 hours and was performed by GPT-6 Astra (the $10/$50 model launched Sept 5).</li>
            <li>The proof itself was produced by an <strong>unreleased next-generation OpenAI model</strong> described in the announcement as "significantly more capable than GPT-6 Astra."</li>
          </ul>

          <p>This is a structurally important point: the proof step and the verification step used different models. OpenAI's announcement is careful to position GPT-6 Astra as the verifier (which is auditable and reproducible via the API) while reserving the actual generator credit for a model they have not yet released. For API consumers evaluating "frontier agent capability," the published $10/$50 GPT-6 Astra rate is the lower bound — the model that did the harder work is not on the price sheet yet.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The cost math: $15M+ at published API rates</h2>

          <p>Pricing GPT-6 Astra on OpenRouter as of 2026-09-09:</p>

          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Tier</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Prompt / 1M</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Completion / 1M</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2">GPT-6 Astra (standard)</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$10.00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$50.00</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2">GPT-6 Astra Pro (standard)</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$10.00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$50.00</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2">GPT-6 Astra (batch)</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$5.00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$25.00</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2">Source: openrouter.ai/api/v1/models, fetched 2026-09-09. GPT-6 Astra created=2026-09-05 on OpenRouter.</p>

          <p>The output token bill alone, priced at the standard rate:</p>

          <pre><code class="text-gray-800">300,000,000,000 output tokens × $50 / 1M tokens = $15,000,000</code></pre>

          <p>That is just the completion cost. The prompt-side bill is not disclosed by OpenAI, but agent workflows typically carry prompt costs of a similar order of magnitude when each agent reads large proof contexts, tool outputs, and prior step state. Even with generous discounting, the run is a seven-figure event. <strong>Noam Brown</strong> (OpenAI reasoning team lead) confirmed on X (Sept 8) that the run cost millions of dollars, with the caveat that "costs will fall fast." The internal next-gen model is not priced at all — it is not on the API.</p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-4">What the cost economics tells about frontier agent workloads</h3>

          <p>Two operational takeaways for anyone running large agent systems:</p>

          <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
            <li><strong>Cache reads matter more than headline rates.</strong> The 17-hour Lean verification is the equivalent of thousands of repeated LLM calls over the same proof context. If your agent architecture cannot cache prefix reads, you pay the full $50/M every time. Anthropic's Claude Fable 5.1 cuts cache reads to $0.25/M (−75% from $1.00/M on Sept 1, 2026); OpenAI's batch tier halves the rate; cached inference discounts on Workers AI and OpenRouter can drop effective costs another 50-90% on long-prefix workloads.</li>
            <li><strong>Multi-model architectures split the bill across rate sheets.</strong> OpenAI's own architecture used a frontier unreleased model for the creative step and a published frontier model (GPT-6 Astra) for the verification step. Anthropic's Haiku 4.5 ($1/$5) and Sonnet 5 ($2/$10) handle routing and verification in many published reference architectures; routing the cheap, repeatable steps through cheaper models can drop a multi-agent cost curve by 5-10× without changing the model that does the hard work.</li>
          </ol>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The concurrent-work dispute with NYU and Anthropic</h2>

          <p>Tristan Buckmaster (NYU mathematician) posted a public statement on Sept 7, hosted at <code>cims.nyu.edu/~tristanb/statement.pdf</code>, describing a year of work with Anthropic researcher Alpöge on AI-assisted fluid dynamics. The timeline he lays out:</p>

          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
            <li>Buckmaster and Alpöge had been collaborating with AI on fluid dynamics problems for over a year.</li>
            <li>In mid-August 2026 they found a counterexample for a simpler version of the Navier-Stokes problem.</li>
            <li>Over subsequent weeks both groups independently worked on the full blow-up problem; communication between the groups was attempted but unsuccessful.</li>
            <li>OpenAI's announcement came on Sept 8 with a public release of the proof.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>Sam Altman publicly responded the same day (Sept 8) with a longer post that argues the Anthropic side had only an Euler result, that coordination between the groups had failed, and that the Anthropic side had threatened a plagiarism complaint. <strong>Noam Brown</strong> and <strong>Daniel Imberman</strong> posted commentary acknowledging the cost (Noam: "costs will fall fast"; Daniel: "improve the model for everyone"). <strong>Emad Mostaque</strong> called the result a milestone toward ASI. <strong>Simon Willison</strong> covered the controversy but stayed neutral on the credit question.</p>

          <p>For the math: <strong>Terrence Tao</strong> posted a Mastodon thread (mathstodon.xyz/@tao/117233527638291447) describing the technical content of the result. Tao has not yet issued a final verdict on the proof; independent review is still pending. A blow-up solution would be a substantive answer to the Millennium Prize question, but the community has not finished checking the proof text. Buckmaster's statement raises procedural concerns about credit attribution that the math side cannot resolve on its own.</p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-4">What the dispute tells API consumers</h3>

          <p>The credit question matters less for the API cost story than the engineering fact it confirmed. Two independent teams, using different institutional infrastructure (OpenAI's frontier unreleased model + Lean 4 verification; NYU's classical training + Anthropic's internal tools), converged on the same problem within weeks of each other. The capability signal — that this kind of multi-agent research workload is now reproducible at multiple organizations — is the durable takeaway, regardless of who gets historical credit for the Millennium Prize.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The two-model architecture: frontier generator + published verifier</h2>

          <p>OpenAI's announcement is explicit that two distinct models participated. The architecture is a useful template for anyone designing agent systems that need auditable outputs:</p>

          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Step</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Model</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Approx. cost basis</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Reproducibility</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2">Proof generation (creative step)</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Unreleased next-gen OpenAI model</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Not disclosed</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No (model not on API)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2">Lean 4 formal verification (17 h)</td><td class="px-4 py-2">GPT-6 Astra</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$50/M output × 17 h</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Yes (OpenRouter / OpenAI API)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2">Multi-agent orchestration</td><td class="px-4 py-2">~10K concurrent agents</td><td class="px-4 py-2">All rolled into the 4.9M / 300B totals</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No (internal coordination)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>The split is the durable design pattern. The creative step (proof, generation, ideation) lives behind a frontier model whose API price is whatever the frontier labs charge at release; the verification step (formal check, unit test, static analysis) runs on a published, cheaper model where every call is auditable and the bill is predictable. The same pattern shows up in production agent deployments that route Haiku 4.5 or Sonnet 5 to the verification side of a Claude pipeline, or that route GLM-5.3 Flash ($0.15/$0.50) to the verification side of a Z.ai workflow.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What this means for the OpenAI API price sheet</h2>

          <p>Nothing changes in the published numbers. GPT-6 Astra remains $10/$50 per 1M tokens on OpenRouter as of 2026-09-09; the batch tier is $5/$25; the standard Pro tier is the same as standard. What changes is the operational envelope:</p>

          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
            <li><strong>Frontier research workloads are a real product tier</strong> — millions of dollars per problem at current rates, with cost trends tracking Noam Brown's "will fall fast" prediction.</li>
            <li><strong>GPT-6 Astra is positioned as the verification layer</strong>, not the frontier creative model — a useful signal for anyone pricing a multi-model deployment: published frontier models are increasingly the audit layer above a more capable internal generator.</li>
            <li><strong>Input-side cost discipline matters more than ever</strong> — at 4.9M messages, even modest prompt-side efficiency wins ($0.50/M cache reads, prompt caching, agent deduplication) compound into six-figure savings on a single research campaign.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>The Clay Millennium Prize committee has not yet ruled on whether the proof satisfies the formal prize conditions; that review can take months or years. For API consumers, the practical question — whether frontier labs can reliably coordinate thousands of agents to produce auditable, verifiable outputs at published rates — has been answered: yes, at seven figures per problem.</p>

        </div>

        <aside class="lg:col-span-1 space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">OpenAI Navier-Stokes — Quick Facts</h3>
            <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Announcement:</strong> Sept 8, 2026 (openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution)</li>
              <li><strong>HN thread:</strong> 1,103 points, 955 comments in 24 h</li>
              <li><strong>Messages:</strong> 4.9M (across all attempted problems)</li>
              <li><strong>Output tokens:</strong> ~300B (cumulative)</li>
              <li><strong>Concurrent agents:</strong> ~10,000 for the resolution</li>
              <li><strong>End-to-end time:</strong> ~88 hours</li>
              <li><strong>Lean 4 verification:</strong> ~17 h via GPT-6 Astra</li>
              <li><strong>Proof model:</strong> Unreleased next-gen OpenAI model</li>
              <li><strong>Verifier model:</strong> GPT-6 Astra ($10/$50 per 1M)</li>
              <li><strong>Output-token bill at GPT-6 Astra:</strong> ~$15M</li>
              <li><strong>Total run cost (Noam Brown):</strong> "millions of dollars"</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What It Means for the API</h3>
            <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-1 list-disc pl-4">
              <li>GPT-6 Astra price unchanged at $10/$50 (batch $5/$25)</li>
              <li>Frontier research workloads = $1M+ per problem at current rates</li>
              <li>Verification-side workloads benefit from cache-read discounts</li>
              <li>Multi-model architecture (frontier gen + published verify) is the durable pattern</li>
              <li>Input-side cost discipline now matters as much as output-side</li>
              <li>Cost curve: Noam Brown's "will fall fast" prediction is the relevant trend signal</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Related Reviews</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
              <li><a href="/tutorials/openai-gpt-6-astra-api-pricing-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">GPT-6 Astra API pricing</a> — $10/$50 base, batch $5/$25</li>
              <li><a href="/tutorials/anthropic-claude-fable-5-1-mythos-5-1-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Claude Fable 5.1 / Mythos 5.1</a> — cache read $0.25/M (−75%)</li>
              <li><a href="/tutorials/anthropic-517b-compute-deal-claude-api-pricing-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Anthropic $517B compute deals</a> — supply-side context for the cost curve</li>
              <li><a href="/tutorials/openai-ipo-api-ecosystem-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">OpenAI IPO 2026</a> — cross-lab IPO context</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Sources &amp; Last Verified</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
              4.9M messages / 300B output tokens / ~10K concurrent agents / ~88 h / 17 h Lean verification quoted from OpenAI's announcement (openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/, Sept 8, 2026); HN thread 1,103 points verified via HN Algolia <code>49613262</code>. Concurrent-work timeline from Tristan Buckmaster's public statement (cims.nyu.edu/~tristanb/statement.pdf, Sept 7, 2026); Sam Altman response and Noam Brown cost commentary from X (Sept 8); Terrence Tao technical assessment from Mastodon (mathstodon.xyz/@tao/117233527638291447). GPT-6 Astra pricing verified live against <code>openrouter.ai/api/v1/models</code> on <strong>2026-09-09</strong>. APIRank does not accept payment for favorable coverage.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-100 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 text-center text-xs text-gray-400">
            Ad Placeholder
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>